Constrain the build environments pip creates for these installs.

Every install below builds from source, and pip resolves each sdist's build
dependencies itself, from PyPI, at build time. Nothing pins those, so a recipe
frozen in 2023 is compiled by whatever setuptools and Cython exist today. Two
of them have moved out from under it.

setuptools 82.0.0 removed pkg_resources, which grpcio 1.53.2's setup.py imports
at the top -- so the build died in "Getting requirements to build wheel" with
ModuleNotFoundError before compiling a line. The cap keeps every build
environment on the same side of that removal, and costs nothing in fidelity:
Pipfile.lock pins setuptools==80.9.0 as a runtime requirement anyway, so this
is the setuptools the package ships regardless. setuptools_scm is capped for
the same reason, and both pins are the ones upstream themselves settled on --
2.3 writes this identical constraints file into its Bazel rule.

Cython is pinned for pymssql alone. Upstream pins it to a fork, installed
straight from git, whose _mssql.pyx carries an unbalanced paren inside an
f-string:

    raise ValueError(f"'encryption' option should be {TDS_ENCRYPTION_LEVEL.keys())} or None.")

Cython accepted that up to 3.1 and rejects it from 3.2 on, with "Unclosed
string literal" at _mssql.pyx:671. The Pipfile already pins Cython==3.0.11,
commented "needed by numpy & pymssql", but a pip build environment never sees
the Pipfile. The pin is deliberately scoped to this one install rather than
added to the shared file: numpy 1.24.2 requires Cython<3.0 to build, so a
global pin would trade one broken package for another.

Note what is deliberately *not* changed here: --no-binary=":all:". The removed
ZZZ_NEW_SPEEDUP_PYTHON_MODULES.patch dropped it to have pip take wheels. The
install step below runs `patchelf --set-rpath` over every .so in the tree,
which would overwrite the $ORIGIN-relative rpaths manylinux wheels use to
reach their bundled libraries -- numpy, Pillow and psycopg2-binary all ship a
.libs directory, and all three would import fine in CI and fail on the
installed system. 2.3 builds every module from source too, on this same
runner, well inside the job's time cap.

--- omd/packages/python3-modules/python3-modules.make
+++ omd/packages/python3-modules/python3-modules.make_v2
@@ -69,6 +69,9 @@
             export PATH="$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR):$$PATH" ; \
             mkdir -p "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)" ; \
             install -m 755 "$(PACKAGE_DIR)/omd/use_system_openssl" "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/git" ; \
+	    echo "setuptools<82.0.0" > "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/constraints.txt" ; \
+	    echo "setuptools_scm<10.0.0" >> "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/constraints.txt" ; \
+	    export PIP_CONSTRAINT="$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/constraints.txt" ; \
 	    $(PACKAGE_PYTHON_EXECUTABLE) -m pip install \
 		`: dont use precompiled things, build with our build env ` \
 		--no-binary=":all:" \
@@ -79,6 +82,8 @@
 		--no-warn-script-location \
 		--prefix="$(PYTHON3_MODULES_INSTALL_DIR)" \
 		git+https://github.com/Checkmk/netapp-ontap-cmk.git@e38ce4ce357ad2ea8f47260c269757f8805bdbce ; \
+	    { cat "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/constraints.txt" ; echo "Cython==3.0.11" ; } > "$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/pymssql-constraints.txt" ; \
+	    PIP_CONSTRAINT="$(PYTHON3_MODULES_WORK_DIR)/pymssql-constraints.txt" \
 	    $(PACKAGE_PYTHON_EXECUTABLE) -m pip install \
 		`: dont use precompiled things, build with our build env ` \
 		--no-binary=":all:" \
